      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "International surveillance data suggests a worldwide epidemic of pediatric obesity regardless of geographic, socioeconomic, racial and ethnic factors. Reversing this epidemic of pediatric obesity will require recognition and understanding of the pathophysiology of the condition to enable us to develop novel therapies as well as taking measures to shift social attitudes and public policies at a global level. Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) deficiency is a rare monogenic disorder of early-onset obesity, which has increased our insight into the important role of the leptin-melanocortin pathway in energy balance. Here, we present a patient with POMC deficiency due to a homozygous c.206delC mutation in the POMC gene. We discuss the recognition of this condition in relation to previous literature with emphasis on the crosstalk between hypothalamic and peripheral signals in the development of obesity and the POMC- melanocortin 4 receptors (MC4R) system as a target for therapeutic intervention."},
